Structure and dynamics of \(\mathbf{Zr}_{6}\mathbf{O}_{8}\) metal–organic framework node surfaces probed with ethanol dehydration as a catalytic test reaction
\(^{1}\)Department of Chemical Engineering, University of California, Davis, CA 95616, USA  \(^{2}\)Department of Chemistry, Chemical Theory Center, and Minnesota Supercomputing Institute, University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, MN 55455, USA
KEYWORDS. Metal-organic frameworks, \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) node, topology, UiO-66, ethanol dehydration
## INTRODUCTION
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous crystalline materials consisting of inorganic nodes and organic linkers. Numbering in the tens of thousands1—prospectively in the millions1—these materials are attracting wide attention as adsorbents for gas separation,2-6 as sensors,7 and as catalysts that offer high densities of accessible, reactive sites.6,8-13 MOFs incorporating \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) clusters as nodes (e.g., UiO- \(.66^{14}\) and UiO\(67^{14}\) , Figure 1a) offer the advantages of chemical and mechanical stability at high temperatures14-16 and node chemistry like that of metal oxides, a widely applied class of catalysts.17,18 MOF nodes such as \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) clusters may be compared with well-known metal-oxide-cluster catalysts such as polyoxometallates,19,20 but unless these are dispersed fully on supports they are often present as ill-defined aggregates. In contrast, the uniformity of MOFs commends them as catalysts incorporating well-defined species that can be understood fundamentally, and also as potentially selective catalysts. Such high selectivity is illustrated21 by the MOF-hosted linear \(\mathrm{Pd}_{4}\) clusters synthesized  in-situ  in  the  anionic  network  of  the  MOF \([\mathrm{Ni}_{4}[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{mpba})_{2}]_{3}]^{4-}\) \(\mathrm{\Delta[Me_{3}m p b a~=~}N,N^{\prime}{-}2,4,6\) -trimethyl1,3-phenylenebis(oxamate)], which catalyze carbene-mediated reactions to make cycloheptatriene. One might anticipate similar selectivity advantages from metal oxide clusters that are MOF nodes, and indeed, the \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) nodes of \(\mathrm{NU}{-}1000^{22}\) have
proven to be uniquely effective catalysts for the hydrolysis of the toxic nerve agent Sarin.23
However, key questions about the catalytic nature of the MOFs remain unanswered, because the ligands on the nodes are not well characterized;16 the ligands on \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) clusters are commonly assigned as hydroxyls,24,27 but some authors have mentioned formates formed in the syntheses.28 Defects such as missing linkers and nodes in MOFs with metal oxide clusters as nodes have been invoked to explain their adsorption and catalytic properties of MOF nodes, and these may be the sites for bonding of ligands.12,23 Much remains to be learned about the catalytic sites on these nodes.
In order to better understand the ligands and catalytic properties of MOFs with \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) nodes, we chose to probe them with ethanol—and with ethanol dehydration as a test reaction— because (i) this reaction occurs on metal oxide surfaces that are comparable to the \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) node faces and (ii) both ethanol and water formed in the reaction product react with these faces and (iii) these MOFs are reported to be stable in alcohol and water.29,30 Ethanol dehydration is a good probe reaction because it makes both ethylene and diethyl ether as products, with selectivity depending on the catalyst structure and reaction conditions31—thus, by comparing the MOF nodes with bulk metal oxides, we sought to identify characteristics unique to the nanoscale nature of the nodes. Further, ethanol itself is technologically important, produced on a massive scale from
biomass\(^{29}\) and used as a feedstock for manufacture of high-purity ethylene,\(^{29}\) a building block for polyolefins,\(^{32}\) and diethyl ether, a commonly used solvent and also a volatile starting fluid for diesel engines.\(^{33}\)
The industrial catalyst for ethanol dehydration is \(\gamma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), and the selectivity for product ethylene or diethyl ether can be tuned by choice of the reaction temperature— \(\sim\!673\mathrm{~K~}\) for ethylene and \(\sim\!513\mathrm{~K~}\) for diethyl ether.\(^{29}\) The mechanism of ethanol dehydration on alumina has been thoroughly and repeatedly investigated for more than 50 years. Kinetics measurement and computational results provide evidence of the separate pathways for formation of the olefin and the ether.\(^{34,35}\)
The results reported here demonstrate that MOFs with \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) nodes, with their high densities of isolated active sites, offer high activities for ethanol dehydration, which selectively gives diethyl ether. And the well-defined structures of these MOFs at the molecular level provide a unique opportunity for understanding the mechanisms of ethanol dehydration and catalyst activity loss.
To unravel the effects on the nodes of (i) linkers, (ii) groups formed from solvents and modulators in the MOF syntheses, and (iii) ethanol, water, and products formed from ethanol and other synthesis components, we used infrared (IR) spectroscopy to identify the ligands bonded to the nodes and \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy of digested MOFs to quantify the information. Moreover, we employed density functional theory (DFT) to quantify the energetic details of binding equilibria and thereby elucidate the reaction pathways. Our results now identify the ligands initially formed on the node vacancies—mostly formate and not hydroxyls—and show how to selectively remove them and activate the MOF with the crystal structure intact. Thus, our results demonstrate the uniqueness of the MOF node surface chemistry, by elucidating the competitive bonding of linkers, reactants, and other components and demonstrating how removal of carboxylate ligands as well as breaking and making of linker-node bonds is essential to provide open sites for fruitful reactant bonding and
catalysis—although too much of this "good thing" can unzip the MOF, which must be avoided.
## EXPERIMENTAL AND COMPUTATIONAL METHODS
Synthesis of UiO-66 with HCl modulator. \(Z\mathrm{r}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}\) \((99.99\%\) , \(0.125\ \mathrm{g},\,0.54\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of concentrated HCl (modulator, 37 wt \(\%\) ) were dissolved in \(15~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF in an 8-dram vial by using ultrasound for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) . The linker precursor benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid \((0.123\;\;\mathrm{g},\;0.75\;\;\mathrm{mmol})\) was then added into the solution and dissolved by ultrasound applied for about \(15~\mathrm{min}\) . The vials were kept in an oven at \(353\ \mathrm{K}\) under static conditions for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . White precipitates were produced, and they were isolated by centrifugation after cooling to room temperature. The solids were washed with DMF three times to remove unreacted precursors and with acetone six times to remove DMF. Then the powder was dried at room temperature.
Synthesis of UiO-66 with acetic acid modulator. \(Z\mathrm{r}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}\) \((0.080\ \mathrm{g},\,0.343\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and \(0.7~\mathrm{mL}\) of acetic acid (modulator) were dissolved in \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF in a \(100~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-capped glass jar by using ultrasound for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) . The linker precursor benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid \((0.057\ \mathrm{g},\,0.343\ \mathrm{mmol})\) was then added into the solution and dissolved by ultrasound for about 5 min. The jars were kept in an oven at \(393~\mathrm{K}\) under static conditions for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . White precipitates were produced, and they were isolated by centrifugation after cooling to room temperature. The solids were washed with DMF three times to remove unreacted precursors and with acetone six times to remove DMF. Then the powder was dried at room temperature.
Synthesis of UiO-67 with HCl modulator. \(Z\mathrm{r}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}\) \((0.067\ \mathrm{g},\ 0.27\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and \(0.5~\mathrm{mL}\) of concentrated HCl (modulator, 37 wt \(\%\) ) were dissolved in \(15~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF in an 8 dram vial by using ultrasound for \(5\,\textrm{m i n}\) . Biphenyl- \(\cdot4,4^{\prime}\) -dicarboxylic acid \((0.09\ \mathrm{g},\ 0.38\ \mathrm{mmol})\) was then added to the solution and dispersed by ultrasound applied for about \(15~\mathrm{min}\) . The vials were kept in an oven at \(353\,\mathrm{~K~}\) under static conditions for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . A white precipitate formed, and it was isolated by centrifugation after cooling to room temperature. The solid was washed with DMF three times to remove unreacted precursors and with acetone six times to remove DMF. Then the powder was dried at room temperature.
Infrared spectroscopy. A Bruker IFS 66v/S spectrometer with a spectral resolution of \(2~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was used to collect transmission IR spectra of power samples. Approximately \(30~\mathrm{mg}\) of solid sample inside a glove box was pressed into a thin wafer and loaded into a cell that served a flow reactor (In-situ Research Institute, Inc., South Bend, IN). The cell was sealed and connected to a flow system that allowed recording of spectra while the reactant gases flowed through the cell at reaction temperature. Each spectrum is the average of 64 scans.
BET surface area measurements. All \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms were measured with a Micromeritics Tristar II instrument. Measurements were performed at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) , with the temperature held constant with a liquid nitrogen bath. Consistency criteria were adapted to choose an appropriate pressure range for BET surface area calculations.
\(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR spectroscopy. Samples were prepared by weighing \(20~\mathrm{mg}\) of MOF into a \(1.5\,\mathrm{\ml}\) vial. A \(600~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) portion of the
digestion medium was then added to the tube. The digestion medium used in this work was \(1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{NaOH}\) in \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). The vials were capped and inverted 2-3 times before leaving the samples to digest over a period of \(24~\mathrm{h}\). This OH-based procedure dissolves only the organic portion of the MOF (linker, modulator, solvent, etc.), while the inorganic component drops out as \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\). After \(24~\mathrm{h}\), the clear supernatant solution was transferred to an NMR tube. Liquid \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR spectra were recorded with a Bruker Avance DPX-500 NMR Spectrometer \((500\ \mathrm{MHz})\). The relaxation delay (d1) was set to \(20\,\mathrm{~s~}\) to ensure that reliable integrals were obtained, allowing for the accurate determination of the relative concentrations of the molecular components. The number of scans was 32.
Powder X-ray Diffraction (PXRD). PXRD patterns were obtained using a Farin optical X-ray Diffractometer Model X'Pert Pro MRD. Measurements were made over a range of \(2^{\circ}\) \(<20<30^{\circ}\) in 0.05 step size at a scanning rate of \(1\ \mathrm{deg/min}\).
Catalytic Reaction Experiments. Ethanol dehydration catalysis was carried out in a conventional laboratory once-through tubular plug-flow reactor at \(473{-}523\ \mathrm{K}\) and 1 bar. The MOF catalyst \((50{-}100~\mathrm{mg})\) was mixed with \(5~\mathrm{g}\) of inert, nonporous \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) and loaded into the reactor in an argon-filled glove box. The feed partial pressures were 10 mbar of ethanol and 900 mbar of helium, with a total flow rate of \(5.6\ \mathrm{mL(NTP)/min}\) (ethanol was carried into the reactor by helium flowing through a bubbler, and all of the gas lines downstream of the bubbler were held at \(393\,\mathrm{~K~}\) to avoid condensation of ethanol and reaction products). Products were analyzed with an online Agilent 6890 gas chromatograph. The ethylene conversions were \(<10\%\), and the reactor was well approximated as differential, determining reaction rates directly.
\(523\ \mathrm{K}\) and \(1\ \mathrm{atm}\).\(^{54}\) All energies in the gas phase are reported in kcal \(\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\).
## RESULTS Ligands on MOF nodes
Node sites that are not bonded to linkers are called defect or vacancy sites, depending on whether they reflect undercoordination relative to the pristine material or not. Capping groups on such sites, together with the linkers, balance the charges on the nodes. Groups derived from solvents and modulators in MOF syntheses may coordinate to the nodes\(^{28}\), but the chemistry is not fully understood. We varied the conditions of synthesis to help identify these groups, characterizing the following MOFs (with the modulator in the synthesis being HCl): UiO-66 and UiO-67, among others, each extensively washed with dimethylformamide (DMF) and acetone and activated at \(393\ \mathrm{K}\) under high vacuum for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\) (Table 1). Formate ligands, derived from decomposition of DMF solvent during synthesis, were identified by their IR spectra (Figures S1–S3 in the Supporting Information, SI) as major node components in all of these MOFs; characteristic bands, with assignments discussed below, were observed in the \(2800{-}3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) range as well as at \(2745~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (with deuterated formate, these bands were observed respectively at 2170 and \(2039~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)). Figure S5, consistent with the identification). The spectra imply various modes of formate-node bonding, as occurs on metal oxide surfaces.\(^{55-57}\) (In earlier work it was suggested that synthesis-derived formate might form on UiO-66 nodes (they used acetic acid as modulator), with a preliminary characterization\(^{28}\). DMF also remains bonded to \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{10}\) nodes in the absence of effective washing, characterized by a \(\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{O}\) stretching at a frequency of \(1656~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (Figure S7). This band disappears after washing with methanol or ethanol at temperatures \(<298\ \mathrm{K}\) and is red-shifted relative to that of physisorbed DMF, which has a
corresponding stretch at \(1667~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). The spectra give no evidence of water on the nodes. Details of the spectra and assignments are presented in the SI.
To quantify our observations, we dissolved samples in a solution of \(\mathrm{NaOH}/\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), destroying the MOFs and analyzing the resulting solutions with \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectroscopy (details in the SI). The data confirm the initial presence of formate and DMF on the nodes (detected as dimethyl amine resulting from the treatment in base) (SI), as well as dissolved linkers, present as carboxylates. The NMR peak areas (Table 1) confirm that formate was a major node surface component under our synthetic conditions and indicate more defect sites per node than previously inferred from thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) previously failed to account for the formate (Table 1).
In prior investigations, IR spectra, \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR analyses of digested samples, and DFT calculations were used to infer that vacancies on the \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) nodes were capped primarily by hydroxo and aqua ligands. Our data make clear, however, that formate is not removed when our washing and activation protocols (which mirror those of earlier work) are followed. We observed that the formate ligands begin to decompose when the temperature is raised to \(473\;\mathrm{K}\) with the samples under high vacuum, and we note recent results showing that treatment of similarly synthesized UiO-66 and NU-1000 with liquid methanol or ethanol at room temperature forms alkoxides on the nodes. We now understand this chemistry on the basis of observations that treatment of the formate-decorated MOFs UiO-66 (HCl) with ethanol vapor at \(423\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and 1 bar forms ethyl formate, identified by gas chromatography (Figure S12 in the SI), as well as ethoxy ligands on the nodes, identified by IR spectroscopy (no catalytic ethanol dehydration products formed at this low temperature). However, treatment of the same MOFs with water vapor does not lead to the displacement of formate under our conditions. Thus, although both experimental and theoretical results show that aqua/hydroxocapped vacancies on \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) nodes can be achieved, it is evident that they are not as prevalent as had been thought and that purposeful synthesis (or serendipitous aging) is required to make them (Scheme 1).
Scheme 1. Topology change on missing linker site of UiO-66 nodes, the initial ligands on as-synthesized sample were originally assigned \(\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{OH}_{2}\) and are reassigned here as formate.
To understand the local structures of formate-functionalized Zr-based materials, we turned to DFT calculations using the M06-L functional and UiO-66 cluster models (details in SI) previously determined to be reliable for structural and spectroscopic predictions. We considered several plausible coordination motifs (Figure S22 and Table S4 in the SI), finding the most energetically favorable structure shown in Figure 1b. The defect surface of that structure (4b in the SI) is capped with a \(\kappa^{2}\)-formate group that bridges the two \(Z\mathbf{r}\) atoms on the vacancy face. We computed vibrational frequencies (Table S6
in the SI) to compare with experiment. In metal oxide materials with coordinated formate, bands in the range of 2800–3000 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) have been assigned to \(\mathrm{C-H}\) stretching, with absorptions in the vicinity of \(2745~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) observed for bulk \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}\) surfaces assigned to combination bands involving symmetric \(\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{O}\) stretching and \(\mathrm{C-H}\) bending. Consistent with these observations of the bulk materials, the computed \(\mathrm{C-H}\) stretching vibrations of the formate ligands (using a standard scaling factor of 0.9765) in our model (Figure 1b) are found at \(2902-\) \(2909~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (Table S6 in the SI). We have not attempted to solve the full vibrational Hamiltonian that would be required to compute combination bands, but we emphasize that the sum of the relevant symmetric \(\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{O}\) stretches and \(\mathrm{C-H}\) bends spans the range of \(2742{-}2749~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (Table S6 in the SI), in agreement with the experimental band at \(2745~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). Because the free energy of formate-capped vacancies is lower than that of hydroxyl-capped vacancies, formate ligands, if they form, they will always be favored as ligands on the nodes. Our results show that HCl in DMF solution (HCl was a modulator in the synthesis) did not remove formate ligands from the nodes during synthesis, and we infer that HCl formed complexes with \(\mathrm{DMF}^{62}\) decreasing the ability of HCl to replace formate ligands bonded to the nodes.
To summarize, in our syntheses (which match those reported in the literature), the vacancies on the nodes of UiO-66 (HCl) and UiO-67 (HCl) are covered mostly with formate, with some DMF. Additional synthetic steps are required for capping the nodes with hydroxo and aqua ligands.
## Ethanol dehydration catalyzed by \(\mathbf{Zr_{6}}\) nodes of UiO-66 and UiO-67
We used a once-through isothermal plug-flow reactor fed with gas-phase ethanol at steady state to investigate UiO-66 and UiO-67 for ethanol dehydration catalysis, with most of the experiments carried out at 1.0 bar and \(523\ \mathrm{K}\). The only products were diethyl ether and water, determined by gas chromatography; reaction in the absence of catalyst was negligible. Conversions in the differential range \((<10\%)\) determined reaction rates directly. Typically, conversions declined with time on stream (Figure S15 in the SI), and we determined initial rates by extrapolating the data to zero time on stream (Figure S15 in the SI). IR spectra of the catalysts show that under the catalytic reaction conditions, formate on the nodes was replaced with ethoxy groups within \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) (Figure S15 in the SI)—before the first measurements of the catalytic conversion.
The initial rate data provide a basis for comparing the catalytic activities of UiO-66 (HCl) and UiO-67 (HCl), each synthesized to give nodes with similar initial node ligand populations (mostly formate, some DMF) (Table 1). Furthermore, various ligand combinations were prepared on the nodes of UiO-66, including formate, methoxy, and terminal OH, to allow a further assessment of the ligand effects on catalysis. IR and NMR data demonstrate that treatment of the UiO-66 nodes initially incorporating formate with liquid methanol at \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) led to the replacement of formate with methoxy, as expected (details in SI). Treatment of the methoxylated nodes with water vapor at \(393\ \mathrm{K}\) led to the replacement of methoxy by hydroxo groups, as expected. The initial rate data characterizing the three UiO-66 samples (Table 2) show that, within error, each had the same catalytic activity, demonstrating that each had taken
on the same topology quickly—before the first catalytic data were obtained. Thus, methoxy and hydroxo groups were removed from the nodes as reactant ethanol formed bound ethoxy groups, which are likely reaction intermediates, and water.60
We also compared the activities of UiO-66 (HCl) and UiO-67 (HCl), each made to incorporate mostly formate groups initially. The MOF particle diameters were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (Figure S13 in the SI), with the average particle diameters, pore diameters (approximate averages, determined from the MOF crystal structures), and node vacancy densities being the following: \(200\ \mathrm{nm}\), 6 Å, 1.62 per
node for UiO-66; and \(800\ \mathrm{nm}\), 8 Å, 1.53 per node for UiO-67. The dependence of the catalytic activity on the MOF particle sizes demonstrates that transport limitations in the catalyst pores are significant. UiO-66 was 3 times more active than UiO-67 and 27 times more active than NU-1000. We therefore draw our essential, quantitative conclusions from just one subset of samples, UiO-66.
Our data leave open the question of whether intraparticle mass transport influences the rate of the catalytic reaction in UiO-66 (and this is a remaining challenge in catalysis by MOFs related to the ones we have investigated because of the difficulty of synthesizing samples that differ only in particle size).
For a further comparison, we tested the catalytic performance of porous high-area \(\gamma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}\) under our standard conditions, \(523\ \mathrm{K}\) and 1.0 bar (details in the SI). Like most metal oxides used in catalysis, these lack structurally well-defined surfaces and incorporate various catalytic sites (including Al and Zr Lewis acid sites and proton-donor OH groups in various surroundings).34,35 Each of these oxides was catalytically active, as expected,31,63 but, in contrast to the MOFs, each had a substantial selectivity for ethylene. The initial activity of UiO-66 was twice that of the \(\gamma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (i.e., the \(\gamma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) after 20 min, Figure S14 in the SI). The activity of \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}\) for ethanol dehydration was much less than that of \(\gamma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (Table 2), as expected.64 The results thus show the catalytic sites on the small MOF nodes are significantly different from, much higher in density than, and more selective than those on the extended surfaces of the oxides.
## Catalysts deactivate as MOFs unzip
\(\gamma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) is a commonly used industrial catalyst for ethanol dehydration to form ethylene, with highly stable catalytic activity and selectivity under our reaction conditions. In contrast, both UiO-66 (HCl) and UiO-67 (HCl) underwent rapid deactivation during catalysis at \(523\ \mathrm{K}\) (Figure S15 in SI). XRD data (Figure S17 in SI) characterizing the MOF catalysts that had been used on stream at \(523\ \mathrm{K}\) indicate that they lost essentially all of their catalytic activity, their crystallinity, and their inter
nal surface areas after only \(4\ \mathrm{h}\) of operation (Table S2 in the SI). When the reaction temperature was decreased to \(473\ \mathrm{K}\), the deactivation of UiO-66 (HCl) was slower (Figure 2), but the MOF nonetheless underwent a marked loss of crystallinity after \(20\ \mathrm{h}\) of reaction. The deactivation of the MOFs resulted because the carboxylate groups of the linkers reacted with ethanol to form esters, unzipping the MOFs, as shown by IR spectra of UiO-66 (HCl), including a sharp band at \(1733\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) assigned to \(\nu(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O})\) bands of esters (Figure S18 in the SI), which steadily increased in intensity when the MOF was in contact with ethanol vapor at \(473\ \mathrm{K}\) for \(1\ \mathrm{h}\).
The numbers of node vacancies determined by \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectroscopy were 1.07 per node and 1.62 per node for UiO-66 (acetic acid) and UiO-66 (HCl), respectively. Lower densities of vacancies on the nodes will help maintain the MOF's crystalline structure, but at the cost of catalytically active sites; incorporation of other modulators (acetic acid) and operation at lower temperatures (473 K) also slow down the deactivation (Figure 2, Table S2 in the SI). These are the first results demonstrating the role of MOF defects and their stability and activity under catalytic reaction conditions. This stability limitation pertains to reactions involving compounds that react with node-linker bonds. Therefore, important conclusions are (1) that the MOFs will be better catalysts for classes of reactions, such as hydrocarbon conversions that do not involve reactants or products jeopardize node-linker bonds and (2) MOFs that are designed to be stable to resist breaking of these
bonds will be more effective than those that are not—thus, for example, MOFs with more linkers bonded to each node will be more stable than those with fewer—and there will be a tradeoff between catalyst stability and catalyst activity.
## Node chemistry and mechanism of ethanol dehydration catalysis
When UiO-66 was synthesized to incorporate both formate and acetate ligands (with acetic acid as a modulator), the catalytic activity initially increased as those ligands were removed by formation of ethyl formate and ethyl acetate, and then the activity slowly decreased, as in the experiments mentioned above (details in the SI). The number of ethoxy ligands on the node vacancies after \(20\,\mathrm{h}\) of catalysis in the flow reactor
followed by evacuation for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(423\,\mathrm{~K~}\) was found to be 1.22 per node by NMR spectroscopy (the data were normalized against linker absorptions; no other ligands remained). Because the initial number of vacancies for UiO-66 (acetic acid) was 1.07 per node (Table 1), we infer that each vacancy supported only a single ethoxy ligand, as suggested before. The \(14\%\) increase in vacancy density as ethanol displaced linker groups from the nodes was slow because of the stability conferred by the ditopic character of the dicarboxylate linkers. The changes in the ligands during the catalytic reaction are summarized in Figure S20 in the SI. The results confirm that formate and acetate ligands blocked the active sites, and they further confirm that ethoxy ligands were the dominant intermediate species in ethanol dehydration.
These data show that the total number of node vacancies gradually increased with time as the ethanol dehydration reaction proceeded, which indicates that ethanol replaced linkers on the nodes and generated more defects. We emphasize that this process was accompanied by the degradation of the MOF structure, indicated by the gradual decrease in its crystallinity in the 20-h reaction, as shown in Figure 2B. We infer that the slow deactivation of UiO-66 (acetic acid) after complete removal of acetate and formate ligands was caused by the MOF degradation.
To broaden the picture of alcohol dehydration catalysis by the MOFs, we also investigated the reactions of methanol and of \(n\)-propanol at \(473\,\mathrm{~K~}\). The data show that UiO-66 (acetic acid) is active for dehydration of both of these alcohols, and the only products besides water were dimethyl ether and di-\(n\)-propyl ether, respectively. We also again observed formate and acetate ligands on the MOF nodes during both dehydration reactions, and these gradually desorbed as methyl formate and methyl acetate during methanol dehydration and as \(n\)-propyl formate and \(n\)-propyl acetate during \(n\)-propanol dehydration. As shown previously, methanol and \(n\)-propanol react similarly with the \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) nodes to form methoxy and \(n\)-propoxy ligands, and we infer that their dehydration mechanisms are similar to the ethanol dehydration mechanism, which we discuss below.
Beyond demonstrating that ethanol dehydration takes place at node sites, we sought to elucidate the mechanism of the reaction to better understand the unique character of the MOF nodes as catalysts. We thus turned to theory, using models of the reaction starting from the evidence of node ethoxy ligands as candidate reaction intermediates, combined with the observation that diethyl ether was the only dehydration prod-
uct. After extensive calculations, we narrowed the field to two cluster models,\(^{68}\) with different densities of defect sites. Model A (Figure 3, left) includes a \( Z\mathbf{r}_{6} \) node with 11 linkers (truncated as four benzoate and seven formate groups) and one open site terminated with ethoxy and ethanol (a single defect). The ethanol molecule was included to better describe the secondary coordination shell. Alternatively, model B (Figure 3, right) incorporates a \( Z\mathbf{r}_{6} \) node with 10 linkers (truncated as three benzoate and seven formate groups) and two adjacent defects.\(^{69,70}\) This model was built from model A, with one additional linker replaced by ethoxy to maintain charge neutrality. This change breaks the symmetry of the node and requires the consideration of various proton topologies, as discussed below. Models A and B, containing one and two defects, respectively, are in line with the number of vacancies per node measured experimentally for UiO-66 (Table 2). In all cases, the models were fully relaxed in the geometry optimization, with only the \( p \)-carbon atoms of the benzoate linkers being kept frozen to mimic the MOF environment.
An accepted mechanism of ethanol dehydration on metal oxides involve bimolecular elimination E2-type transition states to form ethylene and bimolecular nucleophilic substitution \( \mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}}2 \)-type transition states to form diethyl ether.\(^{34}\) We report structures and Gibbs free energies (at \( 523\ \mathrm{K} \)) in kcal \( \mathrm{mol}^{-1} \) at M06-L level (see computational details) for a variety of candidate intermediate and transition state structures on UiO-66 nodes. All species are named with a prefix that indicates the node model employed, A, or B, and transition states (TSs) are labeled with E2 and \( \mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}}2 \) to reflect the structures that lead to the formation of ethylene and diethyl ether, respectively.
Figures 4a and 4b include the structures and Gibbs free energies of the proposed active sites and transition states involving model A (i.e., a UiO-66 node with one defect). For clarity, we use a color code to distinguish the following three groups: ethoxy initially bound to \( Z\mathbf{r} \) (black); ethanol initially bound to \( Z\mathbf{r} \) (blue); and ethanol in the secondary coordination shell (red). We take as zero of energy the structure A-1 which includes ethoxy and ethanol ligands bound to the node surface and one additional ethanol molecule hydrogen-bonded to them. Starting with E2-type transition states, we found A-E2-1 at \( 42.2\ \mathrm{kcal\ mol}^{-1} \), which describes a typical elimination process whereby the deprotonation of ethyl and the breaking of the C–O bond occur simultaneously. The additional ethanol maintains the same hydrogen-bonding pattern as in A-1 and does not participate in the reaction. Next, we considered \( \mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}}2 \)-type TSs to explore the possible formation of diethyl ether. In A-S\(_{\mathrm{N}}2\)-1 (46.1 kcal \( \mathrm{mol}^{-1} \)) the ethoxy performs a nucleophilic attack on the ethyl group with a hydroxo bound to the node as a leaving group. Although the additional ethanol is not directly involved in A-S\(_{\mathrm{N}}2\)-1, it plays a key role in A-S\(_{\mathrm{N}}2\)-2 (46.1 kcal \( \mathrm{mol}^{-1} \)). In the latter case, the ethoxy bound to the node deprotonates the free ethanol, which increases the nucleophilicity of the group and promotes the C–O bond formation. In A-S\(_{\mathrm{N}}2\)-3 (44.3 kcal \( \mathrm{mol}^{-1} \)) the ethoxy bound to the node performs a nucleophilic attack on the free ethanol, and the resulting hydroxo simultaneously takes a proton from the adjacent ethanol, forming water. All of these TS energies are in excess of those previously computed for corresponding reactions on bulk oxides,\(^{34,35,64,71}\) suggesting that the one-defect model A would at best lead to sluggish reactivity, and would moreover be expected to be selective for ethylene as product, contrary to what is observed.
We therefore investigated model B (i.e., a UiO-66 node with two defects). Figures 4a and 4b show a summary of the corresponding structures and Gibbs free energies. Exploring various tautomers, we found B-1 to be the most stable species (zero of energy). B-1 includes two ethoxy groups on the "first" node face, and one ethanol adsorbed to the face of the second missing linker. An additional free ethanol molecule again creates a hydrogen-bonded network involving adsorbed ethanol and ethoxy. B-2 is analogous to A-1, but the "second" missing benzoate is directly replaced by ethoxy, and the first node face is decorated with ethoxy and ethanol. From B-2 the E2-type TS can take place via B-E2-1 with an activation free energy of \(49.6\ \mathrm{kcal\ mol^{-1}}\). For \(\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{N}}2\)-type TSs we found configurations analogous to those in model A: \(\mathbf{B-S_{N}}2\mathbf{-1}\) \((47.8\ \mathrm{kcal\ mol^{-1}})\), \(\mathbf{B-S_{N}}2\mathbf{-2}\) \((45.8\ \mathrm{kcal\ mol^{-1}})\), and, most notably, \(\mathbf{B-S_{N}}2\mathbf{-3}\) \((38.7\ \mathrm{kcal\ mol^{-1}})\). Thus, for model B, formation of diethyl ether via B\(\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{N}}2\mathbf{-3}\) is \(3.5\ \mathrm{kcal\ mol^{-1}}\) more favorable than the lowest-energy pathway identified for formation of ethylene via A-E2-1. Although the computed activation free energy of \(38.7\ \mathrm{kcal\ mol^{-1}}\) via \(\mathbf{B-S_{N}}2\mathbf{-3}\) is still somewhat greater than activation (electronic) energies reported for periodic\(^{34,35}\) and cluster\(^{71}\) models of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), it is less than previous values reported for ethylene formation via cluster \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\).\(^{64}\) On that basis, we propose \(\mathbf{B-S_{N}}2\mathbf{-3}\) as the most likely transition-state structure leading to formation of diethyl ether.
To provide a better understanding of the \(\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{N}}2\) process at the atomic level, Figure 4c displays the lowest \(\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{N}}2\)-type TS structures for both models, \(\mathbf{A-S_{N}}3\mathbf{-3}\) and \(\mathbf{B-S_{N}}3\mathbf{-3}\). In \(\mathbf{A-S_{N}}2\mathbf{-3}\) the leaving water is forming a hydrogen bond with one neighboring ethoxy. In \(\mathbf{B-S_{N}}2\mathbf{-3}\) we observe the exact same hydrogen bond plus an additional interaction with the ethoxy of the second defect site. This point is reflected in the \(\mathrm{C\cdotsOH}_{2}\) distance, which is only \(2.033\ \mathrm{\AA}\) in \(\mathbf{A-S_{N}}2\mathbf{-3}\) but \(2.110\ \mathrm{\AA}\) in \(\mathbf{B-S_{N}}2\mathbf{-3}\).
To summarize, model A with one defect has very high predicted activation energies for ethylene and diethyl ether formation; by contrast, a doubly defective node, model B, which forms by breaking of node-linker bonds, substantially reduces the activation free energy for diethyl ether formation. The unique confined environment of the node leads to a hydrogen bonding network that stabilizes the water co-product (Figure 4c), thus selectively enhancing the formation of diethyl ether. The calculations show that highly defective Zr-containing nodes account for the observed diethyl ether selectivity, doing so by involving an ethanol molecule in the second coordination shell around the active site—that is, through an outer-sphere, micro-solvation mechanism. A key result is that breaking of node-linker bonds (likely a reversible, dynamic process) facilitates the catalysis. The linkers that stabilize the MOF at the same time hinder the catalytic reaction—the linkers are both catalyst stabilizers and reaction inhibitors.
## CONCLUSIONS
The \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) nodes in the MOFs UiO-66 and UiO-67 catalyze ethanol dehydration to give exclusively diethyl ether, in contrast to metal oxides such as \(\gamma\mathrm{-Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\), which give both diethyl ether and ethylene as dehydration products. The catalytic reaction on the MOF nodes is inhibited by ligands such as formate and acetate formed during the MOF synthesis, but these ligands are displaced by ethoxy groups upon expo
sure to ethanol. The key to the selectivity for the bimolecular ether formation reaction, elucidated by DFT calculations, is the adjacency of ethoxy-capped sites, formed by the ethanolysis of node-linker bonds (or existing coincidentally), to allow catalytically fruitful bonding of reactant ethanol to neighboring sites on the nodes. The limited and unique dimensions of metal oxide cluster nodes and their surroundings in MOFs offer new opportunities for catalysis different from that on extended metal oxide surfaces, and the essentially molecular character of these nodes opens the way to powerful guidance by theory, ultimately for catalyst design.
